schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "No legitimate service ever asks for your recovery phrase"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Anyone who reads your recovery phrase controls your wallet completely,
    with no bank to call and no fraud department to reverse a theft — and
    the single rule that defends against nearly every newcomer scam is that
    no legitimate wallet, exchange, or "support agent" will ever ask you to
    type or share it.
  evidence: >-
    Article names three recurring scam patterns as evidence: (1) fake
    support agents claiming an account is "frozen" and requesting the
    recovery phrase to "fix" it; (2) "free-money bait" posts that publicly
    share a wallet's recovery phrase, tricking people into importing it and
    draining their own funds instead; (3) lookalike sites/apps, near-perfect
    copies of real wallets built to capture what's typed.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/what-is-wallet.md
  interpretation: >-
    Positioned as the single highest-leverage safety rule in the article —
    "hold that one line and you've avoided most of the danger" — because it
    covers all three named scam vectors at once.
  uncertainty: >-
    General security guidance stated as settled fact in a plain-language
    guide; no incidence statistics or named source cited for how common each
    scam pattern is.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-wallet.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
